header(s, "PART 1 · THE PROBLEM", "Asphaltenes are a billion-dollar flow-assurance problem", 2, 45, CLK[2])
cards = [
    ("billions $/yr", "estimated industry-wide cost: lost production, remediation, chemicals [1]"),
    ("≈ $70 M", "shut-in and cleanup for ONE deepwater Gulf of Mexico well [1]"),
    ("up to $1.2 M/day", "deferred-production loss during a plugging event [2]"),
    ("1/3 to 2/3", "of tubing radius: measured deposit thickness in severe field cases [3]"),
]
x = 0.62
for big, cap in cards:
    r, t = rect(s, x, 1.80, 2.94, 1.95, fill=LIGHT, line=TEAL, lw=1.2,
                shape=MSO_SHAPE.ROUNDED_RECTANGLE, radius=0.07)
    par(t, big, size=23, bold=True, color=NAVY, first=True, space_after=4, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
    par(t, cap, size=10.5, color=GRAY, space_after=0, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER, line=1.05)
    x += 3.10
tf = box(s, 0.62, 4.00, 12.1, 2.7)
par(tf, "Field quote to set up the talk: when production pressure drops, the heaviest molecules in the crude crash out of solution and build a hard, tar-like deposit that chokes the tubing.",
    size=14.5, first=True, space_after=8)
par(tf, "It is not an exotic failure mode: asphaltenes are managed somewhere in the world every single day — from the North Sea to the Gulf of Mexico to the Algerian Sahara.",
    size=14.5, space_after=8)
par(tf, "The recurring sting is not only the money — it is that plugging arrives unplanned, and the industry's default answers are reactive.",
    size=14.5, space_after=8)
par(tf, "So the question of this seminar: can we predict WHERE it will deposit — before the well chokes?",
    size=15, bold=True, color=NAVY, space_after=0)
notes(s, """TARGET 45 s (clock 1:00).
[VERIFY] Ref [1] = ACS Energy & Fuels review, "Review of Asphaltenes in an Electric Field" (2021): industry-wide billions + $70M/well GoM shut-in. Ref [2] = MDPI Processes review "Mitigation of Asphaltene Deposit Formation via Chemical Additives" (2025): up to $1.2M/day. Ref [3] = Hassi Messaoud field data (SPE-994-PA) and related case papers: deposits ≈ 2/3 of tubing radius.
Delivery: land each number slowly; the $70 M and $1.2 M/day figures do the heavy lifting.""")

# =========================================================
# 3 — WHAT ARE ASPHALTENES
# =========================================================
s = blank_slide()
header(s, "PART 1 · THE PROBLEM", "What are asphaltenes? The heaviest, most polar fraction of crude — held in suspension", 3, 35, CLK[3])
tf = box(s, 0.62, 1.80, 7.4, 4.9)
items = [
    ("Operational definition (not one molecule, but a solubility class):", "insoluble in n-heptane, soluble in toluene — the basis of the SARA analysis (Saturates · Aromatics · Resins · Asphaltenes) [VERIFY]: ASTM D6560 / IP 143."),
    ("Why they stay in the oil:", "aromatic-rich resins act as the stabilising shell around asphaltene nano-aggregates; the crude is stable as long as that dispersion is maintained."),
    ("Why the nickname fits:", "field engineers call them the cholesterol of crude oil — and like cholesterol, the danger is not their presence, it is precipitation from circulation."),
    ("Scale:", "typically a few per cent of the oil by weight — enough to plug a well completely."),
]
for i, (a, b) in enumerate(items):
    par(tf, "▪  " + a, size=14.5, bold=True, color=NAVY, first=(i == 0), space_after=2)
    par(tf, b, size=13.5, color=TEXT, space_after=10, line=1.05)
r, t = rect(s, 8.25, 2.00, 4.45, 3.3, fill=LIGHT, line=TEAL, lw=1.5, shape=MSO_SHAPE.ROUNDED_RECTANGLE, radius=0.06)
par(t, "THE STABILITY PICTURE", size=12.5, bold=True, color=TEAL, first=True, space_after=8, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
par(t, "asphaltene\n(aggregate core)", size=13.5, bold=True, color=NAVY, space_after=2, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
par(t, "↓ surrounded by ↓", size=13, color=GRAY, space_after=2, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
par(t, "resins + aromatics\n(solvating shell)", size=13.5, bold=True, color=TEAL, space_after=8, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
par(t, "Disturb the shell → instability → precipitation", size=12.5, italic=True, color=RED, space_after=0, align=PP_ALIGN.CENTER)
notes(s, """TARGET 35 s (clock 1:35).
Key correction to make explicitly (committees test this): asphaltenes do not "keep the oil stable" — they are kept dispersed BY resins and aromatics.
[VERIFY] Confirm the exact standard number your course uses for the SARA/heptane-precipitation test (ASTM D6560 / IP 143) and cite it on the slide.""")

